A||||11||Philphine||v6 idle air control/ air filter question||||1||
Z||000000||Philphine||09-04-2001||01:05 AM||||<BR>on my 4cyl fiero this is held on with 2 screws. on the v6 it looks like a big plastic nut. dose it screw off? i'm getting a 35 code and my idle won't go below 1300rpm so i want to check it. i don't want to break it in the process of removing it.<P>also i was checking the air filter (this is a car that has been sitting 1 1/2-2 years) and the filter was compleatly wet. condensation from sitting all this time? it dosen't look altered. anything to worry about? thanks for any help.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Joe Torma||09-04-2001||03:55 AM||||Yep, its "a big nut"  unscrew it with whatever wrench/socket works.<P>I guess you should replace that filter!  I dunno if there's anything else you should do.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/simplesig.gif"> <BR>Did you get your car in my book yet????<BR>http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm||||reg||1
Z||000002||Philphine||09-04-2001||09:11 PM||||changed air filter already. it looked too bad to use. i'm just wondering how it got so wet.<P>also got iac off, but i can't find a test for it. i know there is one 'cause i did my 4cyl once a year or so ago but i can't find the procedure. anyone know it? and also which pin gets the 9 volt signal? thanks again for any help.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||09-05-2001||01:15 AM||||The filter getting wet is a problem on V6's.  With the air cleaner right under the engine vent, rain drips down and stands on the top of the filter and runs down inside.  I put a nylon washer under my air cleaner nut, and that helps quite a bit.||||reg||1
Z||000004||LarryB||09-05-2001||05:42 AM||||The quick test for the IAC is essentially the same for the 2.5 or the 2.8L.<P>- Warm up the engine so the ECM is in closed loop mode, then note the idle RPM.<BR>- Shut off the ignition, disconnect the harness to the IAC, and restart the car.  <BR>- note the idle RPM now, shut off the ignition, reconnect the harness to the IAC.<BR>- restart the car again and note the idle for the 3d time.<P>If the first and 3rd idle speeds you noted are the same and the 2nd idle (when the IAC is disconnected) is higher than either of them your IAC is most likely OK and you should start looking for nick or small split in the vacuum hoses and EGR tube.  It doesn't take much of a leak to cause a 1300 RPM idle (been there, done that). <P>If the idle speeds are the same you've probably either got a huge vacuum leak (not likely since your idle now is only 1300 RPM) or a problem with the IAC, the ECM, or the harness that runs between them.<P>Since you've got the IAC off the car already you might as well check the resistance across the coils before reinstalling it. According to the Helms check between where the blue/white and blue/black wires plug in, and then between where the green/white and green/black plug in.  You should measure at least 20 ohms.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Philphine||09-05-2001||09:08 AM||||ok, thanks. i'll try both of those. i thought i remembered also a test where you apply 9v to one of the pins to extend/retract the plunger, but i guess those two tests should give me enough info. if i can find my vaccume guage i'll check that too. thanks again.||||reg||1
Z||000006||LarryB||09-05-2001||11:33 AM||||Unplugging the IAC is the first recommended test to run, because a) it's quick, straightforward, and pretty easy to do and b) it's a pretty reliable indicator if the IAC or related parts are the cause. There are a couple of other tests, including the resistance check, but they all pretty much assume that this first test implies a problem with the IAC.<P>If your results point toward the IAC I can look up and post the other recommended tests if you need it from my Helms.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Philphine||09-05-2001||07:57 PM||||ok, i found the test i was talking about and it failed (plunger twitches but dosen't move). i also did the resistance check and got continuity. i'm not sure about that one. i was using a digital vm and i don't know if 20ohms is enought to get a reading from it or if it would just show continuity. a mechanical one might have shown some scale deflection or something. i also found out i can use my mity-vac to check vaccume so i'll try that tomorrow, but i guess i'm getting a new iac. i'm pretty confident i need to. i hate spending money on parts i don't need. thanks again everyone.||||reg||1
Z||000008||LarryB||09-06-2001||12:08 AM||||20 ohms is more than enough to be measured even by a really inexpensive digital VM, as long as the proper scale was selected (or if it autoselects, the autoselect feature is working properly).  If you don't measure at least 20 ohms resistance the IAC fails the test; just knowing you have continuity doesn't really show anything. <P>It could also be that you were doing the test right and the IAC was just failing it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||AL||09-06-2001||07:02 PM||||philphine is the IAC dirty? you can clean it with break cleaner, and that will let it move freely.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Philphine||09-06-2001||07:46 PM||||that could be. in fact, i got a new one and tested it and it worked the same. that made me check it with a vm again and i got a continuity beep too. then i watched the meter and saw it read at 55ohms and still beeped. the beep is what i went on before so i checked the old one again. 55ohms and a continuity beep. imagine how happy i am with myself now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>oh well. i've bought it so i'm going to use it and just keep the old one as a questionable spare. if it cures the problem i'll feel better about it. if not, i'll just have to add it to the list of lessons learned the hard way. i'll work on it this weekend (work nights) and do the vaccume check then too if needed.||||reg||1
